T* (SECTION 1: Executive Summary) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Australia regulates campaign finance through a layered federal system rather than a single) Tj
T* (national code. The Commonwealth, each state and territory, and \(through state law\) local) Tj
T* (government elections each operate under distinct statutes, definitions, thresholds, and) Tj
T* (enforcement designs. That fragmentation creates a demanding compliance environment for parties,) Tj
T* (candidates, third-party campaigners, associated entities, and donors active across more than) Tj
T* (one jurisdiction.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The contemporary policy direction is toward stronger transparency, tighter controls on source) Tj
T* (and flow of political money, and broader regulation of non-party campaign actors. The most) Tj
T* (significant current national development is the Electoral Legislation Amendment \(Electoral) Tj
T* (Reform\) Act 2025, with key elements commencing from 1 July 2026, including lower disclosure) Tj
T* (thresholds and faster disclosure architecture during election periods.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (At state level, reform intensity has increased in the past decade. New South Wales and) Tj
T* (Queensland have mature cap-and-disclosure systems with strong local-election interfaces;) Tj
T* (Victoria has strengthened disclosure and anti-avoidance design; Western Australia implemented) Tj
T* (major 2023 reform architecture with 2024 operational effects; South Australia has shifted to a) Tj
T* (much more interventionist model through 2024 amendments; Tasmania modernised its framework via) Tj
T* (the Electoral Disclosure and Funding Act 2023; ACT and NT continue to operate structured) Tj
T* (disclosure and expenditure frameworks with jurisdiction-specific settings.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Key unresolved policy questions remain: whether harmonisation should be pursued nationally, how) Tj
T* (to design real-time transparency without disproportionate burden, whether expenditure caps) Tj
T* (suppress or improve electoral fairness, how to manage third-party advocacy without chilling) Tj
T* (participation, and whether local-government integrity risks justify stronger donor-source) Tj
T* (controls in all states.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 2: How to Read This Report) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (This report is structured as a legal-policy reference and should be read with current statutory) Tj
T* (instruments and regulator guidance. Amounts such as disclosure thresholds and caps may be) Tj
T* (indexed or altered by commencement schedules. For legal compliance, readers should verify) Tj
T* (current figures and operative provisions against the latest legislation and regulator notices) Tj
T* (Page 1) Tj
ET
