T* (SECTION 3: Part I: System Architecture and Constitutional Context) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Australias framework is constitutionally plural. The Commonwealth regulates federal elections) Tj
T* (under the Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918, primarily through Part XX \(funding and disclosure\),) Tj
T* (related anti-avoidance provisions, and amendments that address foreign influence and third-) Tj
T* (party campaign activity.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (States and territories legislate separately for their own elections. Local-government election) Tj
T* (funding and disclosure is generally determined under state/territory law, sometimes integrated) Tj
T* (into state electoral finance statutes and sometimes separated through local government) Tj
T* (electoral frameworks.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The High Courts implied freedom of political communication does not prohibit campaign finance) Tj
T* (regulation per se, but it requires proportionality: restrictions must be justified in relation) Tj
T* (to legitimate purposes such as anti-corruption, transparency, and electoral integrity. This) Tj
T* (constitutional dimension significantly influences the design of caps, bans, and third-party) Tj
T* (controls.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 4: Part II: Commonwealth Framework) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Primary legislation: Commonwealth Electoral Act 1918.) Tj
T* (Administrator: Australian Electoral Commission \(AEC\).) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Core elements historically include: annual disclosure by parties/candidates/associated) Tj
T* (entities/significant donors, public election funding tied to electoral performance, and) Tj
T* (treatment of entities incurring political expenditure for federal purposes.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Major modernisation phases:) Tj
T* (1\) Expansion and restructuring of entities subject to obligations \(including third-party) Tj
T* (campaigners and political campaigners\).) Tj
T* (2\) Foreign donation restrictions and associated prohibitions to reduce foreign influence) Tj
T* (channels.) Tj
T* (3\) 20252026 reform package introducing lower thresholds and faster disclosure settings.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Transition point: from 1 July 2026, AEC reform guidance indicates substantial operational) Tj
T* (change in disclosure thresholds, timing, and return structures. For practitioners, the) Tj
T* (transition period requires careful mapping of which rules apply to donations received before) Tj
T* (and after commencement, and whether election-period rapid reporting is triggered.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 5: Part III: State and Territory Regimes) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (New South Wales \(NSW\)) Tj
T* (The Electoral Funding Act 2018 \(NSW\) is one of Australias most developed campaign finance) Tj
T* (systems, with donation caps, expenditure caps, disclosure obligations, and structured) Tj
T* (enforcement. The scheme is deeply relevant to local government elections and has influenced) Tj
T* (broader national debate on cap design and anti-circumvention controls.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Victoria \(VIC\)) Tj
T* (The Electoral Act 2002 \(Vic\), particularly post-reform architecture, establishes disclosure and) Tj
T* (regulatory controls for state elections with anti-avoidance logic and interaction with) Tj
T* (Page 2) Tj
