(integrity measures. Local government campaign finance oversight is not simply a carbon copy of) Tj
T* (state election settings and must be checked against dedicated local rules and oversight) Tj
T* (pathways.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Queensland \(QLD\)) Tj
T* (Queenslands regime spans both the Electoral Act 1992 and Local Government Electoral Act 2011,) Tj
T* (creating an integrated but complex system that addresses donation disclosure, expenditure) Tj
T* (disclosure, caps, and certain donor-source restrictions. Queenslands local election regulation) Tj
T* (is central to its integrity model.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Western Australia \(WA\)) Tj
T* (WAs Electoral Act 1907 has been materially reshaped by recent amendment rounds. Reforms with) Tj
T* (2024 operational impact strengthened disclosure controls and altered obligations for campaign) Tj
T* (participants. Practitioners must check WAEC guidance because transitional detail affects) Tj
T* (practical compliance.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (South Australia \(SA\)) Tj
T* (Through major 2024 amendments to the Electoral Act 1985, SA has moved toward a restrictive) Tj
T* (model with broad prohibitions and carefully delimited exceptions. This positions SA as a) Tj
T* (jurisdiction with stronger direct constraints on private political funding channels than many) Tj
T* (peers.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Tasmania \(TAS\)) Tj
T* (Tasmanias Electoral Disclosure and Funding Act 2023 is a modern dedicated statute, replacing) Tj
T* (older fragmented arrangements and strengthening system clarity.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Australian Capital Territory \(ACT\)) Tj
T* (The ACT Electoral Act 1992 contains developed disclosure and campaign finance settings,) Tj
T* (including explicit treatment of non-party campaign actors.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Northern Territory \(NT\)) Tj
T* (The NT Electoral Act 2004 includes funding and disclosure controls with reporting obligations) Tj
T* (in Part 10, tailored to territory electoral structures.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 6: Part IV: Local Government Elections) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Local government is the least harmonised layer nationwide and often the highest-risk integrity) Tj
T* (environment due to the proximity of electoral activity to land use, procurement, and) Tj
T* (development decisions.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Three operating models are visible:) Tj
T* (1\) Integration model \(e.g., stronger integration in NSW/QLD\),) Tj
T* (2\) Hybrid model \(state electoral controls plus separate local-government gift/disclosure) Tj
T* (rules\),) Tj
T* (3\) Parallel integrity model \(local-government-specific frameworks administered through) Tj
T* (different institutions\).) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Policy attention repeatedly focuses on whether council-level donor restrictions should be) Tj
T* (stricter than state-level baselines, especially where development interests create concentrated) Tj
T* (influence incentives.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 7: Part V: Reform Trajectory \(Historical and Contemporary\)) Tj
T* (Page 3) Tj
