(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (1980s: modern disclosure/public funding architecture matures at Commonwealth level.) Tj
T* (1990s2000s: threshold/indexation and transparency lag become persistent controversy.) Tj
T* (2010s: rising focus on circumvention, associated entities, and third-party expenditure.) Tj
T* (2018 Commonwealth reforms: significant tightening of foreign-donation and campaigner) Tj
T* (regulation.) Tj
T* (2020s state wave: multiple jurisdictions redesign cap/disclosure systems.) Tj
T* (2025 Commonwealth reform statute: prepares major shift commencing 1 July 2026, with lower) Tj
T* (threshold and faster disclosure in election periods.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Reform trend summary: movement from static annual transparency toward dynamic, closer-to-real-) Tj
T* (time reporting and stronger source restrictions.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 8: Part VI: Data, Disclosure Practice, and Evidence) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Disclosure data are typically fragmented across jurisdictions, publication cadences, and return) Tj
T* (formats. Analysts face recurring issues:) Tj
T* (- delayed visibility in annual-return systems,) Tj
T* (- inconsistent donor/entity categorisation,) Tj
T* (- limited interoperability for cross-jurisdiction analysis,) Tj
T* (- compliance under-reporting risk where audit intensity is low,) Tj
T* (- attribution challenges for complex fundraising structures.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Recent reforms seek to reduce these limitations by accelerating publication and expanding) Tj
T* (regulated actor categories. However, administrative burden and data-standardisation constraints) Tj
T* (remain central implementation issues.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 9: Part VII: Research Literature and Policy Debate) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Australian legal and policy scholarship has concentrated on seven clusters:) Tj
T* (1\) transparency quality \(timeliness, completeness, usability\),) Tj
T* (2\) cap effectiveness and unintended consequences,) Tj
T* (3\) constitutional validity and proportionality,) Tj
T* (4\) third-party campaign regulation and democratic participation,) Tj
T* (5\) foreign influence and sovereignty protection,) Tj
T* (6\) public funding design and incumbent effects,) Tj
T* (7\) local-government corruption risk controls.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Across this literature, there is broad agreement on the value of transparency and anti-) Tj
T* (circumvention measures, but less agreement on the optimal strictness of bans and cap levels,) Tj
T* (and on how to balance burden against democratic participation.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (SECTION 10: Part VIII: Practical Compliance Guide) Tj
T* (--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T* (Step 1: Determine legal status \(party, candidate, associated entity, third-party campaigner,) Tj
T* (donor, publisher\).) Tj
T* (Step 2: Determine election context \(federal/state/local; ordinary/general/by-election\).) Tj
T* (Step 3: Identify operative law version and commencement date.) Tj
T* (Step 4: Confirm threshold/cap values and indexation notices.) Tj
T* (Step 5: Implement source-verification and prohibited-source screening.) Tj
T* (Step 6: Establish contemporaneous records for receipts, liabilities, and electoral expenditure.) Tj
T* (Step 7: Build deadline matrix \(annual returns, election returns, expedited event-driven) Tj
T* (Page 4) Tj
